The agent card gives the interface address as a relative path, and the official A2A JavaScript SDK cannot use it.

  GET /tracon/a2a/summarizer/.well-known/agent-card.json
    "supportedInterfaces": [{ "url": "/tracon/a2a/summarizer", "protocolBinding": "JSONRPC", "protocolVersion": "1.0" }]

A client built from that card (`new ClientFactory().createFromUrl(cardUrl, '')` in @a2a-js/sdk 1.3.0) fails on its first
call with `TypeError: Invalid URL`: the transport builds `new URL(interface.url)` and a path is not a URL. The A2A
specification gives `AgentInterface.url` as the URL where the interface is available, which a caller outside the
host's own origin can only use when it is absolute. The .NET SDK the endpoint is tested with resolves a relative address
against the card's own URL, which is why the .NET tests never saw it.

The same card with the address made absolute works end to end in the same run (a message is answered, an unknown task
is a TaskNotFoundError), so the fault is the address alone.

Why it is not fixed here: the card is built once, when the endpoint is mapped, from the pattern only. The host does not
know its public address (a reverse proxy, a custom path base, a container port), and an address taken from the Host
header of the request is one a caller chooses. The fix is a decision: a `PublicBaseUrl` option on the A2A options (the
card then carries an absolute address, and stays relative when it is not set), or an address built from the request
when the host runs the forwarded-headers middleware.
